      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Context",
          "content": "Despite a position as the first-line pharmacotherapy in type 2 diabetes, the glucose-lowering mechanisms of metformin remain to be fully clarified. Gut-derived modes of action, including suppression of bile acid reabsorption and a resulting increase in glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) secretion, have been proposed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Objective",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to assess the GLP-1 secretory and glucometabolic effects of endogenously released bile, with and without concomitant single-dose administration of metformin in patients with type 2 diabetes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Design",
          "content": "Randomized, placebo-controlled, and double-blinded crossover study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Setting",
          "content": "This study was conducted at Center for Diabetes Research, Gentofte Hospital, Denmark."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Patients",
          "content": "Fifteen metformin-treated patients with type 2 diabetes; all participants completed the study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Interventions",
          "content": "Four experimental study days in randomized order with administration of either 1500 mg metformin or placebo in combination with intravenous infusion of cholecystokinin (0.4 pmol × kg-1 × min-1) or saline."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Main Outcome Measure",
          "content": "Plasma GLP-1 excursions as measured by baseline-subtracted area under the curve."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Results",
          "content": "Single-dose metformin further enhanced bile acid-mediated induction of GLP-1 secretion (P = 0.02), whereas metformin alone did not increase plasma GLP-1 concentrations compared with placebo (P = 0.17). Metformin, both with (P = 0.02) and without (P = 0.02) concomitant cholecystokinin-induced gallbladder emptying, elicited reduced plasma glucose excursions compared with placebo. No GLP-1-mediated induction of insulin secretion or suppression of glucagon was observed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusions",
          "content": "Metformin elicited an enhancement of the GLP-1 response to cholecystokinin-induced gallbladder emptying in patients with type 2 diabetes, whereas no derived effects on insulin or glucagon secretion were evident in this acute setting."
        }
      ]},
